    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d718">

                <pb facs="0859" n="785" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v01/pg785"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 860c.01/55</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Sharp</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 5843</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 23, 1917</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received December 10.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith
                    copies of a communication which has recently been handed to me by Mr. R. Dmowski, President of the Polish National
                    Committee having its headquarters at Paris. Of Mr. Dmowski and his associates I have made brief mention in my
                    telegrams Nos. 2591 of October 11 and 2622 of October 19, 1917.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d718-f1"/>
                </p>
                <pb facs="0860" n="786" xml:id="pg786"/>
                <p>Without at this time commenting upon the contents of this communication, it is
                    clearly obvious, both from the matter contained in my telegrams to which
                    reference has been made, and this communication itself, that Mr. Dmowski has not lost sight of the interests of
                    Poland either during or after the war. His views, however, are interesting as
                    coming from one who is very familiar with the political history of Europe, and
                    his observations entitled to careful, consideration.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. G. Sharp</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d718-a1.xml" xpointer="d718-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
